Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** — it hosts your app in-process with a test HTTP pipeline (no real ports).

### Key pieces

1. **`WebApplicationFactory<TEntryPoint>`** creates an in-memory `TestServer` and exposes `CreateClient()` for an `HttpClient` wired to it.

2. For MCP's **Streamable HTTP** transport, your tests send `POST` requests to the MCP endpoint with JSON-RPC payloads and validate the responses (JSON or SSE).

### Minimal example

```csharp
// MyMcpApp.IntegrationTests/McpTests.cs
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using Xunit;

public class McpTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task Initialize_ReturnsServerCapabilities()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0",
            id = 1,
            method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test-client", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        Assert.Equal("2.0", body.GetProperty("jsonrpc").GetString());
        Assert.True(body.TryGetProperty("result", out var result));
        Assert.True(result.TryGetProperty("serverInfo", out _));
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task ToolsList_ReturnsTools()
    {
        // Initialize first (get session token if your server uses one)
        var init = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 1, method = "initialize",
            @params = new { protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" } } };
        var initResp = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", init);

        // Grab Mcp-Session-Id header if present
        if (initResp.Headers.TryGetValues("Mcp-Session-Id", out var vals))
            _client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Mcp-Session-Id", vals.First());

        var listTools = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 2, method = "tools/list",
            @params = new { } };
        var resp = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", listTools);
        resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        var body = await resp.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
        var tools = body.GetProperty("result").GetProperty("tools");
        Assert.True(tools.GetArrayLength() > 0);
    }
}
```

### Setup checklist

- Add `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` NuGet package to your test project.
- Reference your server project (so `Program` is accessible — ensure `<InternalsVisibleTo>` or a `public partial class Program { }` marker).
- Override services in the factory if needed (e.g., swap a real DB for an in-memory one):

```csharp
var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>()
    .WithWebHostBuilder(builder =>
    {
        builder.ConfigureServices(services =>
        {
            // Replace external dependencies with fakes
        });
    });
```

### Why this works well

| Concern | Solution |
|---|---|
| No real TCP port | `TestServer` uses in-memory transport |
| Full middleware pipeline | Routing, auth, DI all run normally |
| SSE streaming | Read `response.Content` as a stream and parse `text/event-stream` lines |
| Fast & parallelizable | No port conflicts, runs in xUnit |

This gives you true end-to-end MCP protocol coverage (JSON-RPC framing, headers, session management) without any external process.
